From 9daa6d93b9b02d3d9ccbb101bb08232d7493c9f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:00:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(update): a core-update pause winds a running
 background review down, and reservations name their holder

Problem (laptop-1 2026-11-13 05:25 PST, drain incident 51354-1794576303896, t_01a1d521213ff881):
the drain window asked 2 turns to pause at 05:25:08 and both parked by 05:25:48, two Kanban workers
kept running, and nothing else was logged until "released timed-out kanban claim hold: claim hold
max_s reached" at 05:28:04 and a 60 m cool-off. The worker reclaim never ran.

Why-chain:
1. hermes-sync's drain_window opens the worker reclaim only when no admission reservation is held.
2. Session f07c32's background review (thread bg-review) held one for the whole window:
   RetirementFence.begin_background_review keeps a locked reservation until the review thread exits.
3. The review ignored the pause request: provider calls #5 (05:26:04) and #6 (05:27:45) started
   after it. Its only stop signal, BackgroundReviewHold.wind_down, was set by process retirement
   after BACKGROUND_REVIEW_WAIT_S (300 s); a core-update drain never set it.
4. Design: the drain's 3-minute claim hold assumes every admission holder parks at a step boundary.
Not caught: reservations carried no owner, so the drain could only log a bare timeout; no test ran a
review together with a worker reclaim.

Fix:
- backend_retirement.review_stop_reason(hold): at every review step boundary
  (turn_iteration_prep.begin_iteration, also re-entered after a preflight compression) a live
  core-update pause request (admission_fence.pause_requested: request AND hold, before expiry) sets
  the review's wind_down with reason update_pause. The review stops before its next provider call,
  never mid-call, and its thread exit releases the reservation.
- run_agent._spawn_background_review_now requeues such a review on the idle queue
  (review_idle_queue.requeue_update_paused_review); past _REVIEW_REQUEUE_MAX_ATTEMPTS, or if the
  queue refuses it, it is dropped with a log line. The queue is in-memory: a backend the update
  restarts loses it (a review is best-effort).
- ReviewIdleQueue dispatches nothing while the update's admission hold is set: a spawn under the
  hold is refused and the requeued review would be lost.
- Reservation.describe(purpose, session) writes a JSON owner record (purpose, pid, session,
  created_at) into the reservation file; reviews, turn admissions and cron ticks label theirs.
  `admission_fence state-details` adds reservation_records (id, purpose, pid, session, age_s). The
  lock is still the claim, so the v1 count protocol and reservation_ids are unchanged; an empty or
  unreadable body reads as an unnamed holder.

Kept: a review that starts while the hold is set is still refused; a retirement wind-down keeps its
exit reason review_retirement_wind_down.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_background_review_update_pause.py (9). RED with the six source files
at origin/main: 6 of the 9 fail (the 3 kept-behaviour tests pass on both sides); GREEN after, with the
existing review-retirement, update-admission, admission-fence, idle-queue and cron-tick suites.
The hermes-sync drain path (fixture: worker + parked chat + live review reservation, and the
"worker reclaim waiting on N admission reservation(s)" log line) is tested in the state PR.
